Incremental clustering is a dead stub — no ordinary write can ever cluster anything; only a full pass clusters, and nothing ever schedules one inline

Reported by `cluster-proof` (relayed by team-lead) from a from-scratch e2e (9/9 green on sqlite, real bge-base-en-v1.5 embeddings, no mocks) proving this at both the pure `clusterStore()` level and the production wrapper. **Independently verified by this agent reading the exact cited code**, not just relaying the claim:

`libs/memory-core/src/cluster.ts:432-441` (read directly):
```ts
const isFullPass = !opts.incrementalOnly && episodes.length <= nodeCap;
if (!isFullPass) {
  // Incremental mode: skip full re-cluster (TODO: local neighborhood check per D1.3)
  return { clusters: [], full_pass: false, unclustered_count: episodes.length };
}
```
When `opts.incrementalOnly` is true, this unconditionally returns an EMPTY cluster set regardless of how many valid, embedded episodes exist — there is no incremental clustering logic at all, just a TODO comment and an early return.

`extensions/bundles/sox-memory-bundle/members/memory-server/src/index.ts:2023-2024` (read directly, inside `runEnrichPassOnDb`):
```ts
const fullPass = await hasPendingFullEnrich(adapter, maxSeq);
const result = await runBatchEnrich(adapter, { incrementalCluster: !fullPass });
```
`fullPass` is true ONLY when an explicit full-enrich row is pending in `organizer_queue` (`hasPendingFullEnrich`). Every periodic tick that ISN'T carrying a pending full-enrich trigger runs with `incrementalCluster: true` — which, per the dead stub above, clusters nothing.

`libs/memory-core/src/curate.ts:374-389` (read directly, `memory_curate {op:'recluster'}` handler, global/non-filtered path): the comment at line 374 states outright "Global recluster (BL-186): enqueue a FULL-pass `enrich` trigger row and let... the MCP recluster call itself can out-wait its client timeout. Deferring to..." — confirmed: `enqueueEnrichFull(adapter, 'memory_curate recluster')` (line 388) only WRITES a queue row; it does not invoke `computeClusters`/`materializeClusters` inline. The row is picked up by the SAME periodic timer on its next tick (matching the already-filed `BUG-MEMORYSERVER-EMBED-HEAL-NOOPERATOR-001`'s description of the same enqueue-then-wait-for-timer pattern for embed healing).

**Net effect, confirmed by tracing the full call chain:** an ordinary `memory_write` never sets a pending full-enrich row; the periodic timer therefore always runs with `incrementalCluster: true`; the incremental path is a stub that returns empty. The ONLY way any clustering ever actually happens is if some other code path explicitly enqueues a full-enrich row (`memory_curate {op:'recluster'}`, or an admin/migration script) AND the next periodic tick picks it up. **No ordinary write-driven usage of this store clusters anything, ever**, absent that explicit trigger.

**Fix sketch:** either (a) implement the incremental-clustering local-neighborhood check the TODO references (per its own citation "D1.3"), so ordinary writes incrementally cluster new episodes without requiring a full pass; or (b) if incremental clustering is not planned, make full passes run automatically on some cadence (not solely on-demand via `memory_curate recluster`) so the feature isn't effectively dead by default; or (c) at minimum, surface in `memory_ping`/`memory_stats` that clustering is currently a no-op for this store's write pattern, so operators aren't surprised by permanently-stale cluster stats. This needs an explicit owner decision, not just a code fix — the TODO has been unresolved since before this incident.

**Acceptance criteria (red→green test naming this item's ID):** a test that (a) writes N episodes via the normal `memory_write` path with no explicit recluster call, waits for/triggers the periodic enrich tick, and asserts TODAY `clusterStats()`/`memory_stats` shows `cluster_count` unchanged and the new episodes uncleustered (red — confirms the dead stub); (b) after the fix (whichever of (a)/(b)/(c) above is chosen), asserts the new episodes are either clustered automatically, or the store's stats/API explicitly report the no-clustering state rather than silently doing nothing.

Citations: [wip/turso-live-metrics, triage, claude, BUG-CLUSTER-INCREMENTAL-PATH-DEAD-STUB, 1: libs/memory-core/src/cluster.ts:432-441 (isFullPass check, empty-return stub, TODO comment) — read directly; 2: extensions/bundles/sox-memory-bundle/members/memory-server/src/index.ts:2023-2024 (runEnrichPassOnDb — fullPass gated on hasPendingFullEnrich, incrementalCluster:!fullPass passed to runBatchEnrich) — read directly; 3: libs/memory-core/src/curate.ts:374-389 (memory_curate recluster op — enqueueEnrichFull only enqueues, does not run inline; comment explicitly describes the defer-to-timer pattern) — read directly; 4: cluster-proof's e2e (9/9 green, sqlite, real bge-base-en-v1.5, no mocks) proving the dead-stub behavior at both clusterStore() and the production wrapper level — relayed by team-lead, not independently re-run by this agent (would require executing tests, out of this agent's investigation-only scope); 5: BUG-MEMORYSERVER-EMBED-HEAL-NOOPERATOR-001 (this repo, this triage round) — documents the identical enqueue-then-wait-for-periodic-timer pattern for the sibling embed-heal path, cross-linked as the same architectural shape]

